VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'As we\'re scanning around us at different distances, at different angles, any slight blare, even if you\'re not aware of it, has to be interpreted by the brain. And the brain essentially fills in the gaps. What that means is your brain as you observe your surroundings'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is essentially operating at a higher rate having to work harder to perceive everything around you. This has been proved by the Z scientists who termed this cognitive load. Think of it as memory on your computer. Your computer has a fixed amount of memory and the'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more processes it\'s engaged in, the slower it gets. When it comes to our brains, they operate in a similar way. The theory is that by improving the clarity of the lens, not just the clarity of the lens in general, so looking directly ahead, but also'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'optimizing more points of clarity throughout the whole lens, by doing both of those things simultaneously, there\'ll be less work rate, less cognitive load on the brain. If that came from any other lens manufacturer, I might be a little bit dismissive. But when it comes'

